		<div class="post" id="post-3724107">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,3724107)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-3724107">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="3122708"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1519777932 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Feb 2018 00:32</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3724107">
									

<p>There are certain elements of this that work for me, and other elements that don't.</p>
<p>The idea of dream pills that allow you to experience fiction with near-eidetic clarity is fun; the idea that it only shares 'public' fiction is also fun &#8212; and the idea that it somehow punishes those who suppress or restrict access to fiction is, while a bit predictable, also potentially interesting.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-3285 will anomalously refill itself when the amount of pills in the bottle has fallen below five.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems unnecessary; why add another anomalous effect to what's already an anomaly? This might be an aesthetic difference, though &#8212; my preference is for anomalies with as few anomalous traits as possible.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subjects affected by SCP-3285-A do not require sustenance to survive, and will awaken at the conclusion of an SCP-3285-A episode without displaying any expected negative physical side effects such as dehydration, malnourishment, or muscle atrophy.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, this seemed like an extra part of the anomaly that didn't contribute to the core story, and served to make the anomaly itself feel less 'credible' to me &#8212; why not just have them make you sleep for 7-8 hours? Particularly given the length of the dreams themselves aren't much longer than that. But, again, this might be chalked up to an aesthetic difference.</p>
<p>I think my bigger issue was with the end. For one thing, it just boils down to them being put on trial and tortured &#8212; I was expecting something along the lines of 'poetic justice' (like them being incapable of perceiving or otherwise consuming any text that's public domain; this includes legal documents &#8212; or them having to experience 8 hours of silence and a 'CENSORED BECAUSE OF COPYRIGHT' screen), but ultimately all the pill does is torture you.</p>
<p>On a broader scale, there's also the fact that I don't know how to feel about any of this: Someone's made a pill that gives us access to fiction in the public domain, but ultimately tortures you for six hours if you've contributed to restricting access to fiction. If the torture had a certain poetry to it, I might feel impressed at their cleverness &#8212; but as it boils down to just a thing that makes you experience pain while putting you through a kangaroo court that tells you how awful you are, all I'm left with is a mild revulsion to whoever created this thing.</p>
<p>TL;DR &#8212; this reads like 'AWCY gone petty and focused exclusively on the absurdity of copyright law'. Pettiness can work, but it's got to be clever &#8212; there has to be an elegance and poetry to the cruelty; otherwise, it starts to feel fetishistic.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Regardless, the polished technical components of the writing &#8212; combined with the creativity of the idea behind 'fiction in pill form' &#8212; is enough to prevent me from downvoting. Good luck with the article!</p>

							</div>

								
					
